                <pb facs="0291" n="284" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg284"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9023: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-28T14:00:00+08:00">July 28, 1937—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 28—7:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>335. The Minister of Foreign Affairs has orally stated to the Embassy that the
                    Chinese Government is anxious to deprive Japanese airplanes of such assistance
                    as they may receive from broadcast weather forecasts relating to China,
                    especially the interior. The Minister understands that such forecasts are
                    broadcast by an American radio station in Manila and rebroadcast by American
                    naval vessels. The Minister inquired whether such weather forecasts could not be
                    terminated or transmitted in code during the present difficulties and asked for
                    an early reply.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
